# OPAC sea-salt complex refractive index, 0.25-40 um, at eight relative
# humidities, for both sea-salt modes.
#
# columns: mode  rh_percent  wavelength_um  n  k
#
# `mode` is OPAC's own component name: ssam is the accumulation mode and sscm
# the coarse mode. They carry DIFFERENT indices at the same humidity because
# OPAC grows each mode separately, so a consumer must ask for the mode it is
# integrating and not assume sea salt has one index.
#
# Hess, Koepke & Schult (1998), "Optical Properties of Aerosols and Clouds: The
# Software Package OPAC", Bull. Amer. Meteor. Soc. 79(5), 831-844,
# doi 10.1175/1520-0477(1998)079<0831:OPOAAC>2.0.CO;2
#
# The paper states these are "not given here as a table". Obtained from
# libRadtran 2.0.6, data/aerosol/OPAC/refractive_indices/, the same route and
# the same release that supplied exoplasim/data/dust/opac_mineral_refractive_index.dat.
# k ships negative in OPAC and the sign is flipped here.
#
# Provenance runs back through d'Almeida, Koepke & Shettle (1991) to Shettle and
# Fenn (1979); the humid values are OPAC's volume-weighted mixture of dry salt
# with water after Shettle and Fenn, using Hale and Querry (1973) for water.
# That mixing rule is what Irshad et al. (2009) find inadequate in the infrared
# (references/irshad2009-sea-salt-refractive-index.pdf), measured from 1 to
# 20 um; their disagreement is in the bands where sea salt absorbs and not in
# the shortwave, where it is a near-pure scatterer and n carries the answer.
